description:
FILED s537b (Opus 5.5, 2026-11-11) from member-1 / member-3 probes.  PRE-EXISTING on main 3d524ad7 (scratch/s537b/pr/m1j.pl m3i.pl m3j.pl m3k.pl in the s537b worktree).

REPRODUCERS (probed vs perl 5.40.3):

    use feature 'state';
    sub f { state %k = (k => 'v'); return "@{[ %k ]}|@{[ scalar(keys %k) ]}" }
    print f(), "\n";                      # perl: k v|1        main: |0   (SILENT)

    my %h = (a => 1);
    package Q;
    print "@{[ %h ]}|@{[ scalar(keys %h) ]}|@{[ $h{a} ]}\n";
                                          # perl: a 1|1|1     main: The variable %h is unbound (raw backtrace)

    my %h = (a => 1); { my %h; }
    package Q;
    print "@{[ %h ]}|@{[ scalar(keys %h) ]}\n";   # perl: a 1|1   main: refuses? no -- prints |0 (SILENT: no span seen)

TWO halves, both "a `%name` inside an interpolation CODE block is a use":
1. The BINDER (Pl::Binder, refs via `interp-code`) records `$h{a}` / `@l` / `$s` inside `@{[ ]}` / `${\ }`, but not a bare `%h` (PCL_BINDER_TRACE=1 on m3i.pl: no ref at line 3; on m3k.pl ONE %h ref for three mentions).  So the span pass never sees the later-section use (no promotion, no refusal) and the capture gate misses it.
2. The rename families' text rewrite `Parser2::_interp_fixer` has no `%name` arm (the `%` arm rewrites `$h{` and `@h{` only) -- the classic `state %k` renames the declaration and leaves `%k` in the code block on the global.

FIX DIRECTION: one reading -- Pl::InterpScan reports the code inside `@{[ ]}` / `${\ }` (it must already delimit it for `$h{a}`); the binder takes every variable token of that code (a re-parse of the block text, like `fragment_doc`), and the fixer rewrites `%name` only inside such a code region (never in plain string text: "100%k" is literal).  Standing rule §8: interpolation scanning happens in Pl/InterpScan.pm.
